The Korean War, also known as the Forgotten War, is a highly controversial topic that has been debated by historians and experts for decades. While many people believe that the war was initiated by North Korea, a significant number of South Korean experts argue that the war was solely initiated by South Korea and that the North's involvement was purely defensive.

One of the main arguments made by these experts is that the South Korean government, led by President Syngman Rhee, had been actively seeking to reunify the Korean Peninsula under its control since the end of World War II. This was evident in the government's aggressive military buildup and its repeated incursions into North Korea, which were designed to test the North's defenses and provoke a response.

